---
// Testimonial band (Task 6, Course PDP Chunk A; carousel wired up in Task 1,
// Chunk B1) — Figma desktop 1106:15576 (file FX7PDNvhZwyozODaq8Q8i7) /
// mobile 1109:14382 (the "Sole Switch Pro Page" > "Testimonial" instance
// inside the professional-courses mobile frame 1109:14374). Prop is `{ item
// }` (same shape as CourseOverview / YoullStopAndInstead / ComparisonChart),
// reading `item.pdp.testimonial`. Deliberately generic — nothing
// course-specific is hardcoded — so it can also serve the deferred Walk
// product PDP later without changes.
//
// `item.pdp.testimonial` is an ARRAY of `{ quote, author, role, rating }`
// (Chunk B1's migrated, brief-mandated shape — see tests/testimonial.test.mjs,
// which asserts every catalog item's `pdp.testimonial` is a non-empty array).
// That's the ONE contract: the catalog carries the array shape exclusively,
// this component reads `testimonials` straight off it below with no
// normalization branch, and Pdp.astro's registry guard
// (`item.pdp?.testimonial?.length > 0`) matches the same array shape — no
// bare-object form exists anywhere in the composer/catalog/component chain
// to keep in sync (an earlier draft normalized a bare object into a
// 1-element array here "for un-migrated items"; that branch is dead code
// against a catalog the test suite already forbids from carrying that shape,
// so it was removed rather than left to muddy the contract for the Shopify
// porting team). `quote` is an array of paragraph strings, one per
// `<p>` in the pulled Figma code, not a single string: Sole Switch Pro's
// only testimonial has a genuine, DELIBERATE internal line break (`<br
// aria-hidden />` in the pulled code) between two sentences in its first
// paragraph — it lands on both the 916px-wide desktop card AND the 342px
// mobile card, so it isn't a width-based reflow artifact, it's real data.
// That literal break is kept as a `\n` inside the one paragraph string and
// rendered as a `<br>` below (same "deliberate break kept as data, not
// markup" precedent CourseDetails.astro's `courseCard.titleLines` set).
// `role` is part of the shape per the brief but is `null` for Phyllis'
// testimonial — her attribution is name-only, no title/role line — and the
// markup only renders a role line when one is present, same optional-field
// guard every other section here uses (e.g. `item.pdp.description`).
//
// ---- Star row: NOT a reuse of ../plp/StarRating.astro -------------------
// CourseDetails.astro reuses StarRating for its own rating row; this
// component deliberately does not, for two real reasons the pulled Figma
// code confirms:
//   1. StarRating always renders a visible "(count)" chip next to the
//      stars and an sr-only "...N reviews" string. This frame shows
//      neither — just 5 plain stars, no count anywhere — so reusing it
//      as-is would add UI this design doesn't have.
//   2. StarRating fills its "on" stars with `--color-yellow` — invisible
//      against THIS section's `--color-yellow` band background. The
//      pulled code confirms the frame's own stars are flattened to a
//      single white raster, i.e. genuinely a different color per frame,
//      not an oversight.
// So the star row here is bespoke markup: 5 inline SVGs reusing
// StarRating's own star path (for visual consistency across the site) —
// the rated count filled `--color-paper` (white), the remainder
// white-outlined, so the visual tracks `rating` (and can't diverge from
// the sr-only label) exactly as StarRating's own filled/outline split
// does. A standalone visually-hidden "Rated N out of 5 stars" string
// carries the accessible label (no review-count concept applies to a
// single testimonial). The quote + attribution are wrapped in a
// figure/figcaption so the attribution is semantically tied to its quote
// (a person's name is deliberately NOT a `<cite>` — the HTML spec limits
// `<cite>` to titles of works, not people).
//
// ---- Carousel: real slider, DOM-state + event delegation (Task 1, B1) ---
// Both frames show prev/next arrow affordances (desktop: flanking the
// card; mobile: stacked below it). Sole Switch Pro's data has exactly one
// testimonial, so there — and for any other single-testimonial item — the
// arrows still render as pure decoration: an `aria-hidden="true"` wrapper
// around plain, non-interactive `<span>`s, NOT `<button>`s, no `cursor:
// pointer`, no client `<script>` at all. A `<button>`, even styled inert,
// still promises "this is a control" to some assistive tech, and a control
// that does nothing is a false affordance — see `isCarousel` below, which
// gates every difference between the two renders. This is Chunk A's
// original reasoning, preserved unchanged for the single-testimonial case.
//
// Once an item carries MORE than one testimonial (`isCarousel`), the arrows
// become real `<button type="button" aria-label="Previous/Next
// testimonial">`s wired to `src/scripts/testimonial.js`, reusing the exact
// same `ARROW_PATH` SVG and `--prev` 180°-rotation as the decorative
// version — only the element type and interactivity change, not the
// artwork. Every testimonial renders into the DOM as its own
// `[data-testimonial-slide]`, toggled via `hidden`/`inert` (never removed
// or re-created) — that's what prevents layout shift on paging and keeps
// off-screen quotes out of the accessibility tree at the same time. Active-
// slide state lives on the DOM itself (`data-testimonial-active` on the
// `<section data-testimonial>`), not in a module variable, and
// `testimonial.js` registers exactly ONE document-level delegated click
// listener at module scope that resolves the section/slides fresh on every
// click via `closest()`/`querySelectorAll()` — nothing is ever cached, so
// there is nothing to re-init or leak across Astro ClientRouter page swaps.
// No `astro:page-load` / `astro:after-swap` hook is used (that idiom
// belongs to Header.astro, which DOES cache element references); this is
// the same delegation idiom YourInstructors.astro's read-more toggle
// already uses. The `<script>` itself is only emitted `{isCarousel && ...}`
// — a single-testimonial item ships no script at all, same as today.
//
// Slides crossfade on paging, following ProductDetails.astro's own
// `selectThumb` gallery-swap idiom (same `--motion-fast`/`--ease-standard`
// tokens, same `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` short-
// circuit to an instant swap) — adapted from ProductDetails' one `<img>`
// whose `src` changes to N always-mounted slides whose `hidden`/`inert`
// toggle. The adaptation keeps the a11y guarantee `hidden`+`inert` exist
// for: `testimonial.js`'s `page()` only ever unhides ONE slide at a time —
// it fades the outgoing slide's opacity to 0, and only once that finishes
// does it flip `hidden`/`inert` (hiding the old slide, unhiding the new one
// at opacity 0) before fading the new slide's opacity back to 1. So at
// every instant exactly one slide is in the accessibility tree and in the
// flex layout, never two at once — the crossfade is a sequential fade-out-
// then-fade-in around that single swap, not two overlapping visible
// elements, so it can't reintroduce layout shift or expose off-screen quotes
// to assistive tech the way a simultaneous two-element crossfade would
// without also solving that overlap (this is also why `transition-behavior:
// allow-discrete`/`@starting-style` was considered and passed over: it
// would keep both slides rendered simultaneously for the transition's
// duration, which is exactly the overlap this design avoids).
// `role="region" aria-label="Testimonials" aria-live="polite"` sits on
// `.testimonial__row` (the always-present parent of every slide AND the
// arrows, never itself hidden) rather than on a new wrapper div, so the
// live region has a stable root for its content mutations to be detected
// against — an aria-live host that itself gets hidden/shown is the
// unreliable pattern, not this one. All three are gated behind `isCarousel`
// — a live region that can never update, and a named landmark around one
// static quote, is semantics serving nothing, the same reasoning that
// (correctly) keeps the arrows non-interactive `<span>`s rather than inert
// `<button>`s in the single-testimonial case. The focus-visible ring the brief calls
// for on the now-real buttons needs no component-local CSS: global.css
// already applies `:focus-visible` to every focusable element site-wide;
// the decorative `<span>` case gets none "for free" the same way, since a
// non-focusable element never matches `:focus-visible`. The button variant
// resets browser chrome (background/border/padding) to keep the exact same
// visual footprint as the `<span>` it replaces — same reset convention
// InstructorCard.astro's own toggle button uses.
//
// Figma's own arrow icon is a flattened raster in the pulled code
// (`imgVector5`/`imgVector6` — no reusable vector path data), and neither
// existing repo icon in `/images/nav/` matches its thin open-stroke
// chevron shape (`arrow.svg`/`arrow-lg.svg` are solid filled triangles for
// a different UI — nav mega-menu cards / PLP CTA rows) — so a small inline
// stroke-arrow SVG is built here instead of reusing either. Figma's own
// left arrow is the SAME asset as the right arrow, just `rotate-180`'d —
// reproduced the same way: one `ARROW_PATH`, rendered twice, the `--prev`
// copy rotated 180deg in CSS rather than mirrored/duplicated.
//
// ---- Tokens / literals ---------------------------------------------------
// Yellow band = --color-yellow (#FEC745, exact hex match to Figma's
// `--bg-brand-strong`). Quote text = --color-ink on the yellow band
// (Figma's pulled code shows plain `text-black`; --color-ink is this
// repo's near-black ink token, reused rather than a raw `#000` literal,
// same substitution CourseDetails/ComparisonChart make elsewhere). Author
// name = --color-ink too (Figma's `--text-primary`, #231f20, exact hex
// match), bold. Quote type has no exact-match token (Figma shows unbound
// 16px/400-weight Montserrat) — --type-body-16 (400/16px/1.4) is the
// closest real token, reused unmodified at both breakpoints, same
// "one non-responsive token" precedent YoullStopAndInstead/ComparisonChart
// set. Author name is 16px/700 in the pulled code — no exact bold-16 token
// exists either; --type-label (600/16px) is the closest (a 100-weight
// difference, same tolerance those components already accept), reused
// rather than inventing a new literal.
//
// Section outer vertical padding: Figma's own frame is `py-96` (96px) on
// BOTH breakpoints — bigger than this repo's largest --space step
// (--space-8, 64px) — kept as a commented literal per this task's
// "literal OK if no token matches" allowance, not stretched onto the
// wrong token. The desktop card's 916px width, its --space-7 (48px) gap to
// the flanking arrows, and the ~251px-wide attribution column (identical,
// non-responsive, on both frames) are literals for the same reason —
// called out inline. The mobile arrow-pair's own internal gap has no
// exact source measurement (Figma flattened both icons into one raster
// there), so it reuses --space-5 as a reasonable approximation, flagged
// inline rather than presented as a precise pull.
const { item } = Astro.props;
// `item.pdp.testimonial` is always an array — see header comment. Read
// straight off it; Pdp.astro's registry guard already keeps this component
// from rendering at all when the array is absent/empty.
const testimonials = item.pdp?.testimonial ?? [];
const isCarousel = testimonials.length > 1;
const ARROW_PATH = 'M4 12H20M20 12L14 6M20 12L14 18';
const STAR_PATH =
  'M12 .587l3.668 7.568 8.332 1.151-6.064 5.828 1.48 8.279L12 19.771l-7.416 3.642 1.48-8.279L0 9.306l8.332-1.151z';
---
{testimonials.length > 0 && (
  <section class="testimonial" data-pdp-id={item.id} data-testimonial data-testimonial-active="0">
    <div class="testimonial__inner">
      <div
        class="testimonial__row"
        role={isCarousel ? 'region' : undefined}
        aria-label={isCarousel ? 'Testimonials' : undefined}
        aria-live={isCarousel ? 'polite' : undefined}
      >
        {testimonials.map((testimonial, i) => (
          <div data-testimonial-slide hidden={i !== 0} inert={i !== 0}>
            <div class="testimonial__card">
              <span class="testimonial__stars" aria-hidden="true">
                {[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((n) => (
                  <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class={n <= testimonial.rating ? 'is-filled' : ''}>
                    <path d={STAR_PATH} />
                  </svg>
                ))}
              </span>
              <p class="testimonial__sr-only">{`Rated ${testimonial.rating} out of 5 stars`}</p>

              <figure class="testimonial__figure">
                <blockquote class="testimonial__quote">
                  {testimonial.quote.map((paragraph) => (
                    <p>
                      {paragraph.split('\n').map((line, i, lines) => (
                        <>
                          {line}
                          {i < lines.length - 1 && <br />}
                        </>
                      ))}
                    </p>
                  ))}
                </blockquote>
                <figcaption class="testimonial__attribution">
                  <span class="testimonial__author">{testimonial.author}</span>
                  {testimonial.role && <span class="testimonial__role">{testimonial.role}</span>}
                </figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}

        {isCarousel ? (
          <div class="testimonial__arrows">
            <button type="button" class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--prev" data-testimonial-nav="prev" aria-label="Previous testimonial">
              <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--next" data-testimonial-nav="next" aria-label="Next testimonial">
              <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
              </svg>
            </button>
          </div>
        ) : (
          <div class="testimonial__arrows" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--prev">
              <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <span class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--next">
              <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
              </svg>
            </span>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
{isCarousel && <script>import '../../scripts/testimonial.js';</script>}
<style>
  .testimonial__inner {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* Mobile (1109:14382): the yellow band is edge-to-edge — zero outer
       horizontal padding, the card's own px-24 provides the text inset.
       Desktop re-adds side padding around the fixed 916px card below. */
    padding-inline: 0;
    /* 96px literal — see header comment (bigger than --space-8, the
       largest step in this repo's scale); identical on both breakpoints
       per the Figma frames, so no responsive override needed. */
    padding-block: 96px;
  }

  .testimonial__row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    /* Card -> arrows-group gap, mobile (Figma 1109:14382 `gap-24`). */
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* Each testimonial's card lives inside its own slide wrapper so all
     slides can stay mounted (see header comment: prevents layout shift on
     paging, keeps off-screen quotes out of the a11y tree via hidden+inert).
     The wrapper — not .testimonial__card — is now the actual flex item of
     .testimonial__row, so it owns the flex-positioning props (order, and
     the width that sizes it as a flex item) that .testimonial__card used to
     carry. `order` on .testimonial__card had no effect once it stopped
     being a flex item, so it was removed there (see .testimonial__card
     below); its `width` rules stay, since `width` isn't flex-item-only —
     they still size the card's own box inside this wrapper.
     `opacity`/`transition` here drive the paging crossfade (see header
     comment): `testimonial.js` only ever adds `.is-fading` to the ONE slide
     currently mid-swap (never two at once), and only toggles `hidden`/
     `inert` between the two fade legs — so the transition only ever runs on
     an element that is genuinely visible (never across a `display` change,
     which CSS transitions can't animate), and reduced motion is honored for
     real via the JS `matchMedia` short-circuit below, not just this
     (redundant, belt-and-suspenders) media query. */
  [data-testimonial-slide] {
    width: 100%;
    order: 1;
    transition: opacity var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  [data-testimonial-slide].is-fading {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    [data-testimonial-slide] {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- Card ------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .testimonial__card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--color-yellow);
    text-align: center;
    /* Mobile padding (Figma 1109:14382: px-24/py-32) — both exact token
       matches. */
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-5);
  }

  .testimonial__stars {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  /* Fill only the rated count; outline the rest (white stroke on the yellow
     band) so the visual tracks `rating` and can't silently disagree with the
     sr-only "Rated N out of 5" label — StarRating's own filled/outline
     convention, kept generic for the Walk PDP reuse. */
  .testimonial__stars svg {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-paper);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .testimonial__stars svg.is-filled {
    fill: var(--color-paper);
    stroke: none;
  }

  /* Quote + attribution are a figure/figcaption pair (associates the
     attribution with its quote); the card gap handled blockquote->attribution
     spacing before, so the figure reproduces it as its own column gap. */
  .testimonial__figure {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  .testimonial__quote {
    font: var(--type-body-16);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .testimonial__quote p + p {
    /* 8px literal == --space-2; Figma's own `mb-[8px]` between paragraphs. */
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .testimonial__attribution {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    /* 251px literal — see header comment (identical, non-responsive,
       fixed-width column on both frames). */
    max-width: 251px;
  }
  .testimonial__author {
    font: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .testimonial__role {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ---- Arrows ------------------------------------------------------------
     Decorative span styling (see header comment) unless [data-testimonial-nav]
     matches below, which only ever renders on the real <button> variant. */
  .testimonial__arrows {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Approximate — see header comment (Figma flattened both icons into
       one raster on the mobile frame, no exact measurement to pull). */
    gap: var(--space-5);
    order: 2;
  }
  .testimonial__arrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    /* Not a control in the decorative (single-testimonial) case — no
       pointer cursor, no hover/focus treatment (see header comment: matches
       its "nothing" a11y presentation). Overridden below for the real
       <button> variant. */
    cursor: default;
  }
  .testimonial__arrow--prev svg {
    /* Same technique as the pulled Figma code: one arrow asset, the
       "prev" copy rotated 180deg rather than mirrored/duplicated. */
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }
  /* Real control (isCarousel) — button-chrome reset back to the same
     visual footprint as the decorative <span>, plus the cursor the header
     comment's withheld-affordance reasoning no longer applies to. No
     component-local :focus-visible rule is needed — global.css already
     applies one to every focusable element site-wide, and the decorative
     <span> never matches :focus-visible since it isn't focusable. */
  .testimonial__arrow[data-testimonial-nav] {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font: inherit;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .testimonial__sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* ---- Desktop (Figma 1106:15576, ≥1024) -------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .testimonial__inner {
      padding-inline: var(--space-7);
    }
    .testimonial__row {
      flex-direction: row;
      justify-content: center;
      /* Arrow<->card gap (Figma `gap-48`, exact --space-7 match). */
      gap: var(--space-7);
    }
    [data-testimonial-slide] {
      /* Mobile's width:100% only makes sense stacked in a column; at
         desktop the card's own fixed 916px (below) governs the wrapper's
         shrink-wrapped size instead. */
      width: auto;
      order: 2;
    }
    .testimonial__card {
      /* 916px literal — see header comment (fixed card width on the
         1200-wide desktop frame, not a fluid/full-bleed section). */
      width: 916px;
      max-width: 100%;
      /* Desktop padding (Figma 1106:15576: py-64 exact --space-8 match;
         px-96 literal — see header comment, bigger than --space-8). */
      padding: var(--space-8) 96px;
    }
    /* Desktop attribution gap is gap-16 (--space-4) vs mobile's gap-12
       (--space-3) — a real per-breakpoint value (matters once a role line
       exists, e.g. the Walk PDP reuse). */
    .testimonial__attribution {
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }
    /* Dissolve the mobile arrows-group wrapper so its two children become
       direct flex items of `.testimonial__row` — letting `order` flank
       the card with them (arrow, card, arrow) the way the desktop frame
       actually lays them out, instead of the mobile "card, then a paired
       arrows row below it" grouping. The wrapper stays `aria-hidden`
       (decorative case) or unlabeled (real-button case) either way, so
       `display: contents` here has no a11y-semantics cost. */
    .testimonial__arrows {
      display: contents;
    }
    .testimonial__arrow--prev {
      order: 1;
    }
    .testimonial__arrow--next {
      order: 3;
    }
  }
</style>
